│ Manual therapy and exercise for adhesive capsulitis (frozen shoulder)                                                │
│                                                                                                                      │
│ Background:                                                                                                          │
│ Adhesive capsulitis (also termed frozen shoulder) is commonly treated by manual therapy and exercise, usually        │
│ delivered together as components of a physical therapy intervention. This review is one of a series of reviews that  │
│ form an update of the Cochrane review, 'Physiotherapy interventions for shoulder pain.'                              │
│                                                                                                                      │
│ Objective:                                                                                                           │
│ To synthesise available evidence regarding the benefits and harms of manual therapy and exercise, alone or in        │
│ combination, for the treatment of patients with adhesive capsulitis.                                                 │
│                                                                                                                      │
│ Selection criteria (apply at title/abstract stage):                                                                  │
│ - Population: Adults with adhesive capsulitis (frozen shoulder).                                                     │
│ - Design: Randomised controlled trials (RCTs) and quasi-randomised trials.                                           │
│ - Interventions: Any manual therapy (mobilisation, manipulation) and/or exercise (supervised or home), alone or in   │
│ combination.                                                                                                         │
│ - Comparators: Placebo, no intervention, a different manual therapy/exercise, or any other intervention.             │
│ - Main outcomes of interest: participant-reported pain relief of 30% or greater, overall pain (mean or mean change), │
│ function, global assessment of treatment success, active shoulder abduction, quality of life, and adverse events.    │
│                                                                                                                      │
│ Screening scope:                                                                                                     │
│ - Title/abstract screening only. Do not use full text.                                                               │
